<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 11</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 11 Walkthrough: Kitchen</h1>

    <p>Kitchen is the eleventh room of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the biggest room the chapter has given you so far: a full working kitchen with an island, a pantry wall, a stove under an arched hood and a breaker panel bolted to the tile. It also has the widest spread of puzzle types in the chapter — a sliding-block puzzle, a colour-sorting letter puzzle, a three-letter lock and a switchboard. This page gives you the two answers you actually have to enter, and is explicit about the parts we could not read.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame at one frame per second. The letter code <b>U A V</b> was read three ways — the whole frame at 2:06, a 4× crop of the lock panel, and a separate 6× crop of each of the three letter windows — and all three passes agreed. The breaker caption <b>TURN ON THE T5 SWITCH</b> was read twice, whole-frame and cropped. Digits that only ever appeared once, or that two passes read differently, are not printed as codes on this page; they are listed under "What this page could not verify".</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={11} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Kitchen is room 11 of 15 in Chapter 3. The three-letter cabinet lock takes <b>U A V</b>; the breaker panel wants the <b>T5</b> switch switched on.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Work the coloured tile-lines above the stove. Same-colour segments assemble into three letters: red <b>U</b>, olive <b>A</b>, dark green <b>V</b>.</li>
        <li>Open the tall pantry cabinet's full-screen sliding-block puzzle, then set the three-letter lock on the cabinet to <b>U · A · V</b> and the door swings open around 2:07.</li>
        <li>At the breaker panel, flip the switch labelled <b>T5</b> — the caption on the panel says exactly that.</li>
        <li>With power back, boil the electric kettle, then collect the mug and doughnuts on the cutting board.</li>
        <li>The digits <b>325</b> printed on that mug are real, but no lock in the footage accepts three digits — treat them as a lead, not a code.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="1tpl1X_K3_Y" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 11 Kitchen Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 11 Kitchen Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/1tpl1X_K3_Y/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 11 Kitchen walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/1tpl1X_K3_Y/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 3:43 Kitchen run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-hero-kitchen.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 11 Kitchen: white cabinets and a light blue wall around a curved island with three stools, a stove under an arched range hood, a tall pantry with a ladder, a fridge on the right, and a wooden door and plant on the left" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>One room, but a room with three working walls. The camera sits high and looks down into the kitchen: white cabinets and a pale blue wall run along the back, an arched range hood sits over the stove, and a curved island with three white stools takes the centre. On the right there is a tall pantry unit with a wooden ladder leaning on it and a two-door fridge beside it; on the left a plain wooden door, a monstera in a pot and a framed picture. The floor is a busy geometric tile pattern with two animal-hide rugs thrown over it.</p>
    <p>The interactive geography is dense. A drawer in the lower cabinets holds cleaning supplies, the sink counter has a bag of bread flour and a bottle of detergent, the backsplash above the stove carries the colour puzzle, the tall pantry has the sliding-block puzzle built into its front, the switchboard is on the tile by the counter, and the island is where the doughnuts and the mug end up at the end. That is a lot of hotspots for one screen, and the recording spends its first minute doing nothing but opening things.</p>
    <p>Structurally the level is a chain rather than a set of parallel locks. Cleaning the walls needs the stove off; the stove is tied to a breaker label; the breaker needs T5; the electric kettle needs the power back. The only straight "type the answer" lock is the three-letter one on the pantry cabinet, and the only place its answer exists is the tile wall two metres to its left.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tall pantry cabinet</td><td>Right wall, ladder leaning on it</td><td>Front face is a <b>sliding-block puzzle</b>; the door carries the <b>three-letter lock</b>. Code <b>U A V</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Three-letter lock</td><td>On the pantry cabinet</td><td>Three letter windows with up/down arrows, one dial each, coloured red, olive, dark green.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coloured tile-lines</td><td>Backsplash above the stove</td><td>Caption: <b>PUT LINES OF THE SAME COLOR TOGETHER TO FORM LETTERS</b>. The clue for the lock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stove and range hood</td><td>Back wall, centre</td><td>Pop-up at 0:46 blocks cleaning until the stove is off; prints <b>(CLUE H7)</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Breaker panel</td><td>Tile wall by the counter</td><td>Eight labelled switches; caption reads <b>TURN ON THE T5 SWITCH</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Electric kettle</td><td>Counter, right of the bowls</td><td>Boils once the power is back; gives the <b>Boiled kettle</b> item.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stacked bowls</td><td>Counter, far left</td><td>Red on top, yellow, green underneath. Colour reference, no lock seen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bottle shelf</td><td>Wooden shelf, left of the hood</td><td>Four bottles with yellow and red caps; sparkles when examined.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sink counter</td><td>Left of the stove</td><td><b>Bread flour</b> bag, vegetables, cutting board, and the <b>Detergent</b> bottle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lower cabinet drawer</td><td>Right of the island</td><td>Opens at 0:26 to cleaning supplies — bucket and cloth.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Handle</td><td>Tall cabinet unit, right</td><td>First inventory item, about 0:14.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Soapy sponge</td><td>Carried item</td><td>In the bag by 1:36; the cleaning tool for the walls.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mug and doughnuts</td><td>Cutting board, near the end</td><td>Two sugared doughnuts and a dark mug printed with <b>3 2 5</b>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:02</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch it</strong>
      The level opens on the full kitchen, no cutscene. Sweep it once: stove and hood on the back wall, pantry and fridge right, door and plant left, island in the middle. The sparkle density here is high, so a single pass tells you how many hotspots you are juggling before you start opening drawers.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-hero-kitchen.jpg" alt="The full Kitchen room at the start of Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 11" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=2s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:02</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:14</span>
      <strong>The Handle comes off the tall cabinet</strong>
      The first pickup is a <b>Handle</b>, taken from the tall cabinet unit on the right. It goes straight into the bag and stays there for a while; the recording opens the inventory at 0:20 to look at it rather than use it.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=14s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:14</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:26</span>
      <strong>The lower drawer holds the cleaning kit</strong>
      A drawer in the lower cabinets, just right of the island, pulls open on a blue bucket, a white cloth and the usual clutter. This is the start of the cleaning thread that the stove pop-up later gates.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=26s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:26</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:38 · the detergent</span>
      <strong>Detergent on the sink counter</strong>
      The yellow bottle standing beside the bread flour bag is the <b>Detergent</b>. With the sponge it forms the cleaning pair. Note the flour bag while you are here — it is scenery in this recording, not a lock.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-sink-flour.jpg" alt="The Kitchen sink counter in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 11 with a bread flour bag, vegetables and a cutting board" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=38s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:38</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:46 · the stove refuses</span>
      <strong>"The stove must be turned off first"</strong>
      Trying to clean the tiles over the stove pops a caption: <b>THE STOVE MUST BE TURNED OFF FIRST, SO THE WALLS CAN BE CLEANED. (CLUE H7)</b>. That is the game telling you two things at once — the order of operations, and the name of a breaker switch. Do not fight it; go and find the switchboard.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-stove-message.jpg" alt="The stove pop-up message in Kitchen reading that the stove must be turned off first so the walls can be cleaned, with the clue label H7" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=46s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 0:54 to 1:24 · the pantry puzzle</span>
      <strong>The sliding-block puzzle on the tall cabinet</strong>
      The front of the tall pantry is a full-screen sliding-tile puzzle: a square frame packed with stone-coloured rectangular blocks and one gap, and you shuffle them until the arrangement clears. The recording spends about thirty seconds here with the clock running, which is the honest signal for how fiddly it is. We are not publishing a move list — the clip is cut and sped up in places, and a half-right sequence would cost you more time than working it out.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-sliding-cabinet-puzzle.jpg" alt="The sliding block puzzle on the tall pantry cabinet in Kitchen, Chapter 3 Level 11" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=54s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:54</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:39 · the colour rule</span>
      <strong>"Put lines of the same color together to form letters"</strong>
      The backsplash above the stove is tiled, and some tiles are coloured. The caption spells out the rule: <b>PUT LINES OF THE SAME COLOR TOGETHER TO FORM LETTERS</b>. Seen cold, the wall looks like broken pieces of an L, an H and a third shape — that is the trap. Read it by colour and each colour resolves into one letter.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-colour-line-letters.jpg" alt="The coloured tile-lines puzzle above the stove in Kitchen with the instruction to put lines of the same color together to form letters" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=99s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:54 to 2:06 · the three-letter lock</span>
      <strong>Set the dials to U · A · V</strong>
      The lock is on the pantry cabinet: three letter windows, each with an up arrow above and a down arrow below, and each window's letter printed in a different colour — red, olive green, dark green. Those three colours match the three colours on the tile wall, which is how you know the letters to dial: <b>U</b>, <b>A</b>, <b>V</b>. In the footage the dials are still scrolling at 1:57 (G-M-N), 2:01 (U-T-N) and 2:03 (U-A-N); they settle on <b>U-A-V</b> at 2:06 and the cabinet opens in the next second.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-letter-lock-uav.jpg" alt="The three-letter lock in Kitchen set to U, A and V, the code that opens the pantry cabinet" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=126s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:07 · inside the cabinet</span>
      <strong>The pantry opens on a bowl, a toaster and the kettle</strong>
      The door swings and the cabinet interior is revealed: a glass bowl, a small toaster and a stainless kettle, with sparkles on the kettle. That kettle is the one that matters later, and it only works once there is power.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-cabinet-open.jpg" alt="The tall pantry cabinet in Kitchen open, showing a bowl, a toaster and a kettle" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=127s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:07</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:36 · the bottle shelf</span>
      <strong>Four bottles, four caps</strong>
      A wooden shelf left of the hood holds four bottles, capped in yellow and red, with a rack of hanging utensils underneath. It sparkles when examined, so the game considers it interactive, but nothing in this room asks you to arrange them and we could not tie the cap colours to a lock.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-shelf-bottles.jpg" alt="The wooden shelf of four bottles in Kitchen, Chapter 3 Level 11" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=156s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 2:56 · the breaker</span>
      <strong>Turn on the T5 switch</strong>
      The panel on the tile wall carries eight breakers, each with its own label printed above it: <b>C5, C6, S2, H7, M3, T5, E2, P4</b>. The caption over the panel reads <b>TURN ON THE T5 SWITCH</b> — so this is a switchboard, not a keypad. Flip T5 and the kitchen's electrics come alive. H7, the label from the stove pop-up, is in the same row; that is the naming scheme the room is teaching you.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-breaker-t5.jpg" alt="The breaker panel in Kitchen with labelled switches including T5, and the caption turn on the T5 switch" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=176s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:56</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:06 · the kettle</span>
      <strong>Boil the kettle once the power is on</strong>
      Back at the counter, the black electric kettle is the first thing that benefits from the breaker. The recording switches it on and the interior glows red; the item then reads <b>Boiled kettle</b> in the inventory. The stacked bowls beside it — red, yellow, green — are the colour reference for the same palette the tile puzzle used.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-kettle-boiled.jpg" alt="The electric kettle boiling in Kitchen after the T5 breaker is switched on, with red yellow and green bowls stacked beside it" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=186s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 3:16 · the mug</span>
      <strong>Two doughnuts, a mug, and 325</strong>
      On a wooden cutting board sit two powdered doughnuts, a spoon and a dark mug of coffee with a printed design that includes the digits <b>3 2 5</b>. Next to the board is a glass sundae. The mug is the last clearly readable clue in the footage. There is no keypad anywhere in this room, so what those three digits unlock is not something we can tell you.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l11-mug-325.jpg" alt="The dark mug printed with the digits 325 on a cutting board next to two sugared doughnuts in Kitchen" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tpl1X_K3_Y&t=196s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:16</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>The tile wall looks like "LHA" and the lock will not take it.</b> That is the commonest wrong turn in this room. The tiles are scattered segments, so the eye completes them into familiar shapes and stops. The instruction is not "read the letters" — it is "put lines of the same colour together". Once you gather the red segments they close into a <b>U</b>, the olive ones into an <b>A</b>, and the dark green into a <b>V</b>.</p>
    <p><b>You cannot find a keypad.</b> There isn't one. The only typed answer in Kitchen is letters, not digits, and it lives on the pantry cabinet. The switchboard is the other "code" and you operate it by flipping T5 rather than entering anything.</p>
    <p><b>The cabinet is jammed and the lock is not even reachable.</b> The sliding-block puzzle on the pantry front has to be cleared first. There is no skip and no hint that solves it for you; budget real time for it, because the recording spends over half a minute there and the clock in the corner keeps running.</p>
    <p><b>The stove will not let you clean.</b> That is intended. The pop-up is the level's order-of-operations gate, and the label inside the brackets — H7 — is a breaker name, not a code to type. Get the power sorted and come back.</p>
    <p><b>The kettle does nothing.</b> It has no power until T5 is on. If you found the kettle before the breaker, that is why it sits inert in the cabinet.</p>

    <h2>Where Kitchen sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and its fifteen rooms run from the Dining Room through the house and out into the garden: Dining Room, Living Room, Study Room, Guest Toilet, Workshop, Guest Bedroom, Master Bathroom, Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden, <b>Kitchen</b>, Driveway, Kids Bedroom, Storage Room, Garage. Kitchen is room <b>11 of 15</b>, and it sits immediately before the Driveway.</p>
    <p>It is also the chapter's widest room mechanically. Earlier rooms stick to one or two code locks; Kitchen hands you a physical puzzle, a colour-sorting puzzle, a letter lock and a switchboard, and then chains them so that the cleaning thread cannot finish until the power is back. If you have just come from Garden, expect the pace to change: Garden is mostly outdoors and mostly reading, while Kitchen is indoor, cluttered and hands-on.</p>

